Purpose:
This registry defines and controls the verification evidence required for
the patient-specific cardiac digital-twin finite-element simulations.

The registry records the current evidence status of:

1. Patient-specific geometry integrity
2. Geometry reconstruction accuracy
3. Finite-element mesh quality and convergence
4. Nonlinear solver convergence
5. Residual error
6. Failed-element assessment
7. Energy-balance assessment
8. Boundary-condition consistency
9. Gravity-condition consistency
10. Output extraction integrity
11. Patient × gravity result traceability
12. Verification-to-manuscript linkage

This document is an evidence-control registry.

It does not create historical COMSOL execution evidence that is no longer
available.

======================================================================
1. VERIFICATION PRINCIPLE
======================================================================

The verification hierarchy used throughout this repository is:

SPECIFIED
    ->
EXECUTED
    ->
VERIFIED
    ->
MANUSCRIPT-REPORTABLE

A documented verification claim is not automatically a verified result.

A numerical value appearing in a recovered workbook, figure, table,
presentation, manuscript draft, or narrative document is classified as:

DOCUMENTED / REPORTED

unless the corresponding underlying computational or source artifact can
be identified and independently checked.

A verification status must therefore be supported by an identifiable
artifact whenever possible.

======================================================================
2. STAGE 3 VERIFICATION SCOPE
======================================================================

Stage 3 verification concerns the computational integrity of the
patient-specific finite-element cardiac digital-twin simulations.

The controlled verification chain is:

Patient imaging
    ->
Segmentation
    ->
Surface reconstruction
    ->
Patient-specific ventricular geometry
    ->
Geometry quality control
    ->
Fiber-field assignment
    ->
Finite-element mesh
    ->
Mesh quality / convergence
    ->
Material and boundary-condition assignment
    ->
Gravity loading
    ->
Nonlinear solver
    ->
Residual / convergence assessment
    ->
Failed-element assessment
    ->
Energy assessment
    ->
Primary output extraction
    ->
Result traceability

Stage 3 verification does not constitute biological or clinical
validation.

It establishes computational integrity and numerical robustness of the
modeling workflow.

